{"text":"Recent evidence favors evaluating individual claims against source evidence. Kabongo and Guigue’s 2025/2026 study reports high semantic similarity but low factual precision in recommendation explanations; its review-derived ground truth is unavailable here, but atomic-claim verification can be adapted to titles, descriptions and histories. Fok and Weld’s 2024 analysis similarly argues that explanations help when users can verify recommendations. [Factual consistency study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24366); [verifiability analysis](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/aaai.12182).

Proposed diagnostic taxonomy, rather than an established validated taxonomy: prerequisite unverified, explicit incompatibility, potential redundancy, and insufficient evidence. Extract prerequisite requirements from descriptions; absence from history means unknown ownership, not a missing prerequisite. Flag incompatibility only with explicit conflicting specifications and an observed relevant historical item; history alone cannot establish current equipment ownership. Detect potential redundancy through matching titles, editions, identifiers or overlapping bundle contents; similarity alone cannot establish an unwanted repeat purchase. Store supporting text spans, historical item IDs and timestamps for every flag. Abstain from assigning a substantive reason when evidence is insufficient.

Validate these proposed probes against the twenty users’ actual feedback, retaining their stated reasons separately from automated flags. Report reason counts, agreement, precision among assessable cases, and coverage; examine errors manually and treat this small sample as exploratory. Abstention should concern diagnostic claims while preserving required recommendation outputs. Do not infer diversity/conformity traits or replace macro validation HR@10 with proxy scores. A relevant 2026 preference-consistency paper, PURE, was withdrawn over feature-extraction and semantic-matching evaluation problems—its reported gains should not support the taxonomy. [Withdrawal notice](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.03080)."}
